114
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:56,000
So for graduation requirements,

115
00:07:56,000 --> 00:08:12,000
Students need a minimum of 80 credits towards their graduation, which can, it's a combination

116
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:19,000
of curricular courses that are required as well as elective courses.

117
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:23,000
It's just lagging a little bit.

118
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:26,000
So we'll just hold on there.

119
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:30,000
So for the graduation requirements, students look at the graduation requirements that start

120
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:32,000
in grade 10.

121
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:38,000
Grade 8 and 9 courses are the courses that help build onto the courses for grade 10.

122
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:43,000
So to graduate with 80 credits, students need to have 12 required courses.

123
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:45,000
You need English 10, 11, 12.

124
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:48,000
You need socials 10, 11, or 12.

125
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:49,000
Science 10, 11.

126
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:51,000
Math 10, 11.

127
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:53,000
Physical and health education 10.

128
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:54,000
Career life education.

129
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:58,000
And then you need to have one course in either fine arts or applied skill between grade

130
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:03,000
10, 11, and 12, as well as seven elective courses that can be either from courses taking

131
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:06,000
in school or courses taken from external credits.

132
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:12,000
And in that, you have to have four courses that are at the grade 12 level.

133
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:15,000
Students then also need to take one Indigenous-focused class.

134
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:21,000
This can be either fulfilled by students taking English First Peoples 10, 11, or 12, as well

135
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:24,000
or taking BC First Peoples 12.

136
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:30,000
These also take Career Life Connections, also known as CLC 11 and 12.

137
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:34,000
Then in grade 10, students will end up writing two assessments.

138
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:39,000
So you write a graduation literacy 10 assessment and a graduation numeracy 10 assessment.

139
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:44,000
So for those of you that are currently in grade 10, you'll be writing that in January

140
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:45,000
during the semester turnaround.

141
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:50,000
And then grade 12, you end up writing a graduation literacy 12 assessment, and grade 12 will

142
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:51,000
write that in November.

143
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:56,000
If you're currently in grade 12 and you didn't write the 10 and numeracy and the 10 literacy,

144
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:58,000
you'll write that in January.

145
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:07,000
Students can also, any student can actually get credits for external credits.

146
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:11,000
So that can be for external activities that you're involved in outside of school.

147
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:13,000
This can be something like a language challenge exam.

148
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:17,000
So an email went out a few weeks back that there's language challenge exams that the

149
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:21,000
Delta School District offers that offers to all the districts to write.

150
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:26,000
And there are specific second languages that are many home languages.

151
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:29,000
Students who might be in certain levels of music or dance.

152
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:35,000
So for example, if you play piano in grade six, seven or eight, you can get external credits

153
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:36,000
for some youth leadership.

154
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:41,000
If you're on a provincial sports team or a BC Summer Games or a national team, you might

155
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:43,000
qualify for external credits.

156
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:46,000
We always let students know to come and see us in counseling to let us know because there's

157
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:54,000
so many different types of credits that are out there that can be put on your transcript.

158
00:10:57,000 --> 00:11:01,000
There are three different types of post second, three main types of post secondary options

159
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:04,000
that we always let families know.

160
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:06,000
So the first being that there's universities.

161
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:10,000
So universities actually kind of go into three different types of universities.

162
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:12,000
There's a traditional research university.

163
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:14,000
So that's your research intensive.

164
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:21,000
That's what you're thinking of your UBC, your SFU, your UNBC, University of Toronto, McGill.

165
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:25,000
Those are no, those have professional programs, undergraduate degrees, graduate degrees.

166
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:29,000
Then there's what's called teaching intensive universities.

167
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:33,000
These are where you see like Capilano University, Portland Polytechnic University, Thompson

168
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:36,000
Rivers University, the Fraser Valley.

169
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:38,000
This is these offer.

170
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:43,000
They offer some certificates, some diplomas, some undergraduate and graduate degrees, but

171
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:49,000
they also offer amazing opportunities for students to go to either do university transfer

172
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:52,000
or offer upgrading opportunities or specialty programs.

173
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:54,000
And then there's known as the specialty universities.

174
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:58,000
These are those niche program universities that offer bachelor's degrees and master's

175
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:01,000
degrees for specific programs.

176
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:05,000
So these are when you're thinking of the Emily Carr or University of Art, the, you know,

177
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:10,000
M.F.C.R. University of Art Design or Royal Roads University.

178
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:12,000
Then there's colleges.

179
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:17,000
So colleges encompass the same as universities in their courses, their credits and their

180
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:18,000
credentials.

181
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:22,000
That means that university professors have to have the same credentials when you're teaching

182
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:24,000
at college and in university.

183
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:28,000
They differ in the university in sense of class size.

184
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:30,000
So they're smaller than university cost.

185
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:34,000
You're about 60% of university and admissions.

186
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:36,000
Some colleges do offer bachelor's degrees.

187
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:42,000
They offer certificates, diplomas, trades, upgrading opportunities and university transfer.

188
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:45,000
So this is like your Douglas, your Langara, your BCC.

189
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:47,000
And then the last is institutions.

190
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:49,000
And this focus more on the technical and trades.

191
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:54,000
So we're looking at BCIT, Justice Institute at BC, Nicola Valley.

192
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:58,000
They do offer some bachelor's degrees, some certificates, some diplomas and master's degrees.

193
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:04,000
So those are like the three different types of encompasses in post-secondary.

194
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:06,000
So for university applications.

195
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:11,000
So when students apply to most universities across Canada, students actually choose two

196
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:14,000
different faculties to apply to because you choose your first choice and then you choose

197
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:15,000
your backup.

198
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:21,000
So for example, as a student who might be applying to faculty of applied sciences, so

199
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:24,000
engineering, that might be their first choice with the backup of science.

200
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:27,000
Or students that might be applying for faculty of sciences or first choice, they might have

201
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:29,000
faculty of arts as their second.

202
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:34,000
It's really important though, and the universities let us know this, to not choose the same faculty

203
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:36,000
is both your first and your second choice.

204
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:39,000
Because if you're not accepted to the faculty, their first choice, they're not going to,

205
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:41,000
it's not going to be a better chance to get into it for a second choice.

206
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:46,000
Unfortunately, there are students that do choose both of them, first and second choice.

207
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:49,000
It's also really important than the universities have let us know it's really important that

208
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:53,000
students meet all the prerequisite admission requirements for both the first and second

209
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:54,000
choice.

210
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:59,000
And the other thing is that faculties that are highly competitive.

211
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:00,000
It's important that you have that as your first choice.

212
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:04,000
So for example, if you're if you are really interested in faculty of engineering, faculty

213
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:08,000
of applied sciences for engineering, or faculty of business, it's really important to have

214
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:10,000
that as your first choice.

215
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:14,000
Because if it's your second, those two faculties are so in demand that if it's your second,

216
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:19,000
they won't look at that because they were already looking at the first choice.

217
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:24,000
BC college is so most colleges are accepted on a first come first serve basis.

218
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:29,000
So we recommend for students to apply online as soon as possible to secure their spot.

219
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:31,000
Many colleges open their applications October 1.

220
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:34,000
So for the grade 12 currently, they're already open.

221
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:38,000
Some program college program acceptance is based on just basic graduation, where some

222
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:40,000
do have course requirements.

223
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:45,000
So for example, you may need a minimum mark in English 12, or English 12 first peoples.

224
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:48,000
And the other great thing is that college also offers upgrading opportunities.

225
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:54,000
So some colleges do offer some grade 12 courses maybe you weren't able to take in high school.

226
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:58,000
Now college university transfer, we know that there's some myths out there about college

227
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:00,000
university.

228
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:05,000
So what that means is university transfer means you start studying at college, and then

229
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:07,000
you transfer to university to complete your studies.

230
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:10,000
So university recognizes the education that you took at college.

231
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:14,000
So for example, if you did two years at college, and then you did two years at university,

232
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:17,000
you gobble up your all your credits, you get your bachelor's degree.

233
