00:32:34,000 --> 00:32:39,000
One of the main questions is, does my AP exam, how does the AP exam score work?

532
00:32:39,000 --> 00:32:42,000
Like, is that related to my class grade in any way?

533
00:32:42,000 --> 00:32:44,000
And the answer to that is no.

534
00:32:44,000 --> 00:32:47,000
So the AP exam score is separate from the course grade.

535
00:32:47,000 --> 00:32:51,000
So the grade you get in class is given to you from your teacher that goes on your high

536
00:32:51,000 --> 00:32:53,000
school transcript.

537
00:32:53,000 --> 00:32:58,000
And then your AP exam score is sent to your university once you begin attending that university

538
00:32:58,000 --> 00:33:01,000
and you can then use that for your university credit.

539
00:33:04,000 --> 00:33:06,000
Can students just take one AP class?

540
00:33:06,000 --> 00:33:10,000
Yeah, I think that's one of the main advantages of AP compared to some other programs like

541
00:33:10,000 --> 00:33:13,000
IB, if you're familiar with that.

542
00:33:13,000 --> 00:33:16,000
It's not a program where you have to take every course at an AP level.

543
00:33:16,000 --> 00:33:19,000
You can take one, you can take two, it's very flexible.

544
00:33:19,000 --> 00:33:24,000
So you can take, you know, many or just one.

545
00:33:24,000 --> 00:33:26,000
Do all Canadian universities accept AP?

546
00:33:26,000 --> 00:33:27,000
Yes.

547
00:33:28,000 --> 00:33:32,000
So what I would recommend if you know what university you plan on attending is just do

548
00:33:32,000 --> 00:33:33,000
a quick search.

549
00:33:33,000 --> 00:33:38,000
If you search UBC, for example, and then advanced placement, each university will have a page

550
00:33:38,000 --> 00:33:44,000
explaining how they transfer the credits over, which courses match up with which AP classes

551
00:33:44,000 --> 00:33:49,000
at that school and how they convert different exam scores to different percentages at that

552
00:33:49,000 --> 00:33:51,000
particular university.

553
00:33:51,000 --> 00:33:56,000
So that would be a really good search to do once you've got your university nailed down.

